hello and welcome to the Daily climate  show coming up today  the fight to over fossil fuels  campaigners are already warning the next  Global Summit might fail to reach a deal  on phasing out coal and gas  the government scrapped a vote on  planning rules after dozens of  conservative MPS publicly threatened to  Rebel as they try to protect Greenfield  sites  in developing a green future as the red  car blast furnace makes way for one of  the UK's first carbon capture and  storage sites  foreign  [Music]  to the Daily climate show where we track  the changes to our world and interrogate  some of the potential solutions to The  Climate crisis  now environmental campaigners and think  tanks say they are concerned about the  prospects for a deal on fossil fuels at  the cop 28 climate change Summit one of  the biggest issues under discussion is  how much help is needed for small and  developing nations to adopt renewable  energy despite calls for an  international deal to speed up that  transition there was no agreement in  shamal Sheikh and now there's concern  there might also be no deal at cop28  which is due to take place in the United  Arab Emirates in a year's time so let's  speak to Alden Mayer who's a senior  associate at the e3g environmental Think  Tank Alden thank you so much for talking  to us so one of the big things that came  out of cop27 was the number of oil  lobbyists just how many were how many  more were they at cop27 and at cop26 and  why is that an issue  well there were over 600 uh fossil fuel  industry Representatives according to  ngos that were tracking this that was up  from cop26 and of course they were not  there to advance climate action and cut  emissions and get us off of fossil fuels  far from that they were there to block  language in the text that would do that  as well as to make trade deals around  the side particularly trade deals  between African countries and Europe to  try to accelerate exported natural gas  to displace Russian gas a lot of people  have said that they are concerned about  the influence that oil producing  countries are having on these talks just  how concerned are you about that and how  much of an influence do they actually  have  well they've had an influence since the  beginning of this process in 1991 the  Saudis the kuwaitis Iran and others have  done everything they could to block  action on climate change matter of fact  in Glasgow last year at cop 26 it was  the first time that any fossil fuel had  been formally mentioned in a co-op  decision as needing to be phased down  and of course that was restricted to  Coal the oil and gas Lobby kept that  from being expanded to include gas and  oil as well  is this problem do you think the fact  that cop is a un Summit and so we look  at cop and see the influence they may  have and it's just a reflection that  they have at the U.N  well the cop has some good attributes  it's the only Forum where the vulnerable  country is the least developed countries  other smaller countries really get a  seat at the table and can use their  moral influence but of course the Saudis  and kuwaitis since the beginning 30  years ago have blocked any decision on  super majority voting in the process so  it requires consensus which gives them  in a handful of others the ability to  block progress a lot of action is  happening outside the cop in the G20 and  the clean energy ministerial and other  spaces that gives me some hope but the  fossil fuel countries and lobbyists do  exercise disproportionate power within  the UN system  if you're looking at geography  geopolitics where this cop is a lot of  people are saying okay it's going to be  in the UAE that's a huge Gulf petrol  state  but cop 26 Cop 27 27 was at charmele  Shake A lot of these all producing  companies were there there wasn't much  change in what was said is it down to  the geography or is it down to the  influence do you think it's unfair I  suppose is what I'm asking for people to  say where it is is a problem  well it's it's both I mean it's  obviously the influence of the fossil  fuel Industries in countries around the  world in the run-up to these annual cops  that influences their position but the  presidency does matter the Egyptian  presidency worked overtime to keep the  option of expanding coal phase down to  include oil and gas out of the cover  decision in charmel shape I think the  UAE may have learned from that not to be  as untransparent as the Egyptians were  and the UAE of course hosts the  international renewable energy agency so  it's open to clean energy sources but  the king of the UAE did say the  beginning of the first week at the cop  that they intend to keep producing oil  and gas as long as there's an demand for  it so we'll see how they play the king  of Saudi  the king of UAE yeah  yeah but if you look at the UAE and  Saudi I mean they are putting a lot of  money into sustainability into working  to combat  uh climate change they probably would  say that that is quite an unfair  representation of what they're going to  do at cop28 and what they're trying to  do in general  well they have made some progress in  recent years on expanding support for  clean energy Technologies of course they  talk a lot about carbon capture and  storage which would allow them to keep  exporting fossil fuels it's not really  an economically viable technology for  large-scale use the real question is  they have been trying to block any push  to phase down production and use of oil  gas and coal as the intergovernmental  panel on climate change says we have to  do if we're going to have any chance of  coming close to the 1.5 Celsius  temperature limit okay Alden thank you  so much for talking to us  good to be with you thanks  now the government has postponed a major  vote on plans to build more houses after  conservative back benches threatened a  large Rebellion more than 40 Tory MPS  signed an amendment to the leveling up  Bill which would make it easier for  councils to ban building on Greenfield  land and provide more incentives to  develop Brownfield sites the former  environment secretary Teresa Villas has  said that without this amendment the  plans would encourage quote development  which damages the environment and  quality of life let's speak to our chief  political correspondent John Craig who  joins me now hello to you John so what  are the government's plans let's start  with that  well the very latest from the government  from astounding Street spokesperson this  afternoon is that that commitment uh to  build uh no fewer than 300  000 new homes uh targets by the  mid-2020s that commitment remains uh  says Downing Street uh they say that we  want to build more homes in the right  places and yet the government has  retreated uh following the threat of a  major Rebellion by up to 50 conservative  MPS now Teresa Villas a former cabinet  minister uh tabled a new Clause to Mr go  Michael gove's Flagship Bill the  leveling up and regeneration Bill and  her new Clause proposed that that Target  of 300 000 may only be advisory not  mandatory she was backed by all sorts of  senior Tory MPS John Redwood Maria  Miller Damien green Chris Grayling  pritchie Patel Esther McVeigh a clutch  of cabinet ministers there so whilst the  government appears to be in Retreat over  a vote that was due to take place next  Monday then that Downing Street is now  saying they remain committed to that  Target of 300 000. incidentally over the  past year the government's only Built a  172  000 so way short of that  okay John thank you so much  well also making the news today work on  what could be one of Britain's biggest  climate change projects are taking  another step forward the 365 foot tall  Blast Furnace at redcast steelworks was  demolished early this morning the site  will now be used for the Net Zero T side  project which aims to build one of the  UK's first large-scale carbon capture  and storage facilities taking greenhouse  gases out of the atmosphere and locking  them underground  Thames water have announced the hose  pipe ban imposed on 15 million people in  England is being lifted after three  months the company says the recent  rainfall and the Public's compliance are  reasons for removing the ban the  restrictions came into effect on August  24th after a drought was officially  declared across most of England  following the driest July for 50 years  Paris is considering a ban on electric  scooters after concerns around  pedestrian safety and questions over  their environmental credentials it was  the first city in Europe to open up the  scooter hire Market four years ago it  has about 15 000 of them for hire and  one councilor has said the vehicles have  turned Paris into a quote jungle  and more than half of the gas lamps in  London's West End will be saved after a  change of Hearts by local officials  Westminster Council had proposed to  convert 174 Street lamps from gas to the  more environmentally friendly LED  Heritage campaigners say the proposals  do not go far enough and claim they are  still fearful about the lamp's future  now if you have a question about climate  change that you want to be answered send  them all via this QR code QR code on the  screen and we will try to answer as many  as possible on the climate Show with Tom  Heap at the weekend and just a reminder  you can watch Tom's show on Saturdays  and Sundays it's at 3 30 in the  afternoon and then again at 7 30 in the  evening but for me Kimberly Leonard  thanks for watching for now and I'll see  you tomorrow  thank you

